[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Java: Reflection, Dynamic Proxies, and Method Handles"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides powerful mechanisms to inspect, modify, and execute code dynamically at runtime. This tutorial explores three advanced pillars of the Java platform: Reflection for inspecting types, Dynamic Proxies for intercepting interface method calls, and Method Handles for performant, direct method invocations."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Java Reflection"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection allows your program to examine classes, interfaces, fields, and methods at runtime, without knowing their names compile-time. It is heavily used in frameworks like Spring and Hibernate."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("java.util.ArrayList");
Constructor<?> constructor = clazz.getConstructor();
Object listInstance = constructor.newInstance();
Method addMethod = clazz.getMethod("add", Object.class);
addMethod.invoke(listInstance, "Hello Reflection");
System.out.println(listInstance.toString());"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Dynamic Proxies"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A dynamic proxy class is a class that implements a list of specified interfaces at runtime. It routes all method invocations to a single invocation handler. This is the foundation of AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) and lazy-loading in ORMs."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public interface Service {
    void execute();
}

public class ServiceImpl implements Service {
    public void execute() {
        System.out.println("Executing service logic...");
    }
}

// Usage with Proxy
Service target = new ServiceImpl();
Service proxy = (Service) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
    target.getClass().getClassLoader(),
    target.getClass().getInterfaces(),
    (proxyObj, method, args) -> {
        System.out.println("Before method: " + method.getName());
        Object result = method.invoke(target, args);
        System.out.println("After method");
        return result;
    }
);
proxy.execute();"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Method Handles"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Java 7, Method Handles (java.lang.invoke) provide a low-level, highly performant mechanism for finding and invoking methods. Unlike traditional reflection, Method Handles are integrated tightly with the JVM, allowing for better compiler optimizations and type safety checks."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"MethodHandles.Lookup lookup = MethodHandles.lookup();
MethodType methodType = MethodType.methodType(int.class, int.class, int.class);
MethodHandle handle = lookup.findVirtual(Integer.class, "sum", methodType);

// Note: Integer does not have a sum instance method, let us use Math.class instead
MethodHandle mathHandle = lookup.findStatic(Math.class, "max", MethodType.methodType(int.class, int.class, int.class));
int result = (int) mathHandle.invokeExact(10, 20);
System.out.println("Max value: " + result);"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"Summary & Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Reflection primarily for configuration, dependency injection, and tooling where compile-time types are unknown."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Dynamic Proxies when you need to intercept interface-based method calls for cross-cutting concerns like logging, security, or transactions."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Method Handles when performance is critical, such as writing custom runtime environments, dynamic languages on the JVM, or heavy functional pipelines."}]
